Category: Knowledge Lifecycle
Purpose:
  Defines the full knowledge extraction pipeline that transforms raw
  observations into trust-weighted references, and the promotion policy
  that moves knowledge between lifecycle tiers. This is the operating
  specification for how .sdd/ knowledge is discovered, normalized,
  validated, and linked into the knowledge graph.

Owns: ./

Extraction Pipeline:
  1. TMP to OBSERVATIONS
     Raw source code, external repositories, and project files are
     copied into .sdd/tmp/. AI scans for patterns, anti-patterns,
     and decisions. Each observation is tagged @tmp.* and recorded as
     an @insight.* entry in .sdd/insights/.

  2. OBSERVATIONS to CONCEPT EXTRACTION
     Repeated observations (3+ occurrences by default, or 1 occurrence
     for security-sensitive concepts) are clustered by semantic
     similarity. Each surviving cluster becomes a @candidate.* concept.
     Single observations remain @insight.* entries.

  3. CONCEPT EXTRACTION to SEMANTIC CLUSTERING
     Clustering groups candidates by domain, pattern family, and
     context. Overlapping clusters are flagged as candidate merge
     targets (see CANONICALIZATION.sdd merge protocol).

  4. SEMANTIC CLUSTERING to CANONICALIZATION
     Each surviving cluster is canonicalized: a canonical_name, aliases,
     definition, and primary reference are assigned. The anti-chaos
     rule applies — AI MUST reuse an existing concept before creating a
     new one.

  5. CANONICALIZATION to RELATION DETECTION
     Typed edges (see RELATIONSHIP-VOCABULARY.sdd) are established
     between the new canonical concept and existing concepts/references.
     conflicts-with and related-to edges are flagged for review.

  6. RELATION DETECTION to REFERENCE CREATION
     Validated candidates become @skill.*, @pattern.*,
     @architecture.*, or @best-practice.* references in
     .sdd/references/. Trust, confidence, evidence_count, and
     provenance metadata are attached per KNOWLEDGE-MODEL.sdd.

Knowledge Graph Wiring:
  After reference creation, AI links the new reference everywhere it is
  relevant: to concepts, to project tasks, to CASE specs, and to existing
  decisions. The link replaces copy — documentation stays human-readable
  while carrying a @reference pointer.

Promotion Policy:
  CANDIDATE to VALIDATED:
    Triggered when evidence is gathered: passing tests, completed
    tasks, or human review. Requires at least 2 independent evidence
    sources. AI proposes; a human (or automated gate) must approve.

  VALIDATED to ESTABLISHED:
    Triggered when the reference is successfully applied across 2+
    distinct projects without regression. Requires production or
    project-level evidence. Human approval mandatory.

  Rules:
    [P1] Promotion is NEVER automatic — always requires evidence.
    [P2] CANDIDATE to VALIDATED requires human approval.
    [P3] ESTABLISHED requires multi-project success evidence.
    [P4] Downgrades (ESTABLISHED to VALIDATED to CANDIDATE) are
         permitted when evidence is invalidated; original evidence
         is preserved.
    [P5] Deprecated references retain history and a pointer to
         their replacement.
    [P6] Obsolete references are NEVER deleted — archived with
         status.deprecated.

Consolidation:
  At task end, Working Memory is consolidated via:
    DROP     — temporary observations with no reuse value
    ARCHIVE  — candidates that did not validate, kept for history
    LINK     — new knowledge linked to existing references
    PROMOTE  — validated knowledge promoted to references/

  Golden Rules (see GOLDEN-RULES.sdd for full list):
    [G1] Never learn silently — every observation is recorded.
    [G2] Never delete knowledge because it is unused — deprecate first.
    [G3] Prefer linking over copying.
    [G4] Prefer reuse over creation.
    [G5] Prefer evidence over assumption.

Garbage Collection:
  - temporary (@tmp.*)           : expires 7 days after last reference
  - candidate (@candidate.*)     : expires 30 days without promotion
  - validated (@skill.* etc.)    : persistent, review every 6 months
  - established                  : long-term, review annually

Knowledge Drift Detection:
  AI compares stale references against current code and documentation.
  When the skill description no longer matches implementation practice,
  a "review required" flag is raised and the reference is marked
  drift.stale until human review.

